WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.291
Rate limiting and request controls protect availability, fairness, cost, and backend resources.

2
00:00:06.291 --> 00:00:07.615
In ASP.

3
00:00:07.615 --> 00:00:13.243
NET Core applications, different requests can have very different operational impact.

4
00:00:13.243 --> 00:00:23.639
A lightweight status check is not the same as a report export, file upload, expensive search, authentication attempt, or tenant-wide synchronization request.

5
00:00:23.639 --> 00:00:28.672
These controls help reduce accidental overload and abusive request patterns.

6
00:00:28.672 --> 00:00:40.525
They can slow repeated requests, protect expensive endpoints, preserve shared capacity for other users, and prevent one client or tenant from consuming disproportionate resources.

7
00:00:40.525 --> 00:00:51.516
They also make the application easier to operate because high-volume conditions become visible instead of remaining hidden inside general latency or failure symptoms.

8
00:00:51.516 --> 00:00:58.204
Application-level rate limiting is not a complete solution for every large distributed traffic event.

9
00:00:58.204 --> 00:01:09.196
Some conditions must be handled at infrastructure layers such as CDNs, gateways, load balancers, hosting platforms, network controls, and managed protection services.

10
00:01:09.196 --> 00:01:20.850
The application still benefits from local controls because it understands endpoint cost, user identity, tenant boundaries, and business intent better than a generic edge layer.

11
00:01:20.850 --> 00:01:25.088
Request controls should be designed around application behavior.

12
00:01:25.088 --> 00:01:41.510
Teams should ask which endpoints are expensive, which clients are expected to call them, which users or tenants share backend resources, what user experience is acceptable when limits are reached, and what operators need to see in logs and metrics.

13
00:01:41.510 --> 00:01:45.152
A control that is too broad can frustrate normal users.

14
00:01:45.152 --> 00:01:49.787
A control that is too loose may not protect the resource that matters.

15
00:01:49.787 --> 00:01:56.011
The practical goal is a request-control baseline that is narrow, observable, and maintainable.

16
00:01:56.011 --> 00:02:09.851
Rate limits, body size limits, connection limits, timeouts, upload workflows, and decompression controls should work together with authentication, authorization, logging, monitoring, and production governance.
